    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-20875",
      "problem": "Plot the curve of $y=8^{x+2}-4.$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y=8^x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/b4d4c39839113d060215b8660886aece5f1d825bf849aee72381b84168ee9f8c.png)\n\n- To plot $y=8^{x+2},$ we shift the graph of $y=8^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/aa831d056ebf71397d4153de957289cb815faeb1f5730c6db9546f2a29a4cc4f.png)\n\n- To plot $y=8^{x+2}-4,$ we shift the graph of $y=8^{x+2}$ vertically by $4$ units down. The point $(-2,1)$ moves to $(-2,-3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/0658340d3dc848575da1c5bf0d30c2ec2f2fca0e7db1633c5274059eed16f3cd.png)",
